[LINK: (Gerda Arendt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Gerda_Arendt]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Gerda_Arendt] )  [LINK: (21:59, 6 December 2025 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Gerda_Arendt-20251206215900-Es_hat_sich_halt_eröffnet] .[ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]

Not a review, but given that this is talking about the events of a song, wouldn't this violate  [LINK: (WP:DYKFICTION) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:DYKFICTION] ?  [LINK: (Narutolovehinata5) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Narutolovehinata5]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Narutolovehinata5]  ·  [LINK: (contributions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Contributions/Narutolovehinata5] )  [LINK: (03:23, 7 December 2025 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Narutolovehinata5-20251207032300-Gerda_Arendt-20251206215900] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]
It is fact about the song being that old and from where (and interesting that from two areas now in different countries, and even then disconnected). The rest is a translation of things from the first stanza, to say what someone not reading German could not know. It adds interest, - I don't know about any other song imagining the little angels doing somersaults (rolling over, see below), and it connects to the image (which isn't brilliant but has a similar idea). Sadly, it hasn't been translated into English, and is so much (cute) dialect that translation programs won't help. - Listen, it's fun ;) - Thanks to  [LINK: (Michael Bednarek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Michael_Bednarek]  who brought that into our Lilypond. Other hook ideas welcome, of course. -- [LINK: (Gerda Arendt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Gerda_Arendt]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Gerda_Arendt] )  [LINK: (08:42, 7 December 2025 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Gerda_Arendt-20251207084200-Narutolovehinata5-20251207032300] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]

This appears to be at an impasse, so giving some hook suggestions here. One of the hooks is misleading, but it might work as an  [LINK: (April Fools) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:DYKAPRIL]  hook if you are willing to have this run on April Fools instead of Christmas. However, it might also work as a quirky.

ALT1 ... that  [LINK: (Karl Marx) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl_Marx_(composer)]  made an arrangement of  [LINK: (a Christmas carol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Es_hat_sich_halt_er%C3%B6ffnet] ? (The link to Marx could be removed if this runs on April Fools)

ALT2 ... that " [LINK: (Es hat sich halt eröffnet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Es_hat_sich_halt_er%C3%B6ffnet] " is the only known Christmas carol in the  [LINK: (Swabian German) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swabian_German]  dialect? (This would require modifying the article and stronger sourcing per  [LINK: (WP:DYKHOOKCITE) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:DYKHOOKCITE]  due to it being a superlative hook)

ALT3 ... that a  [LINK: (hallelujah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hallelujah]  was added to the Christmas carol " [LINK: (Es hat sich halt eröffnet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Es_hat_sich_halt_er%C3%B6ffnet] " after it was criticized for being too secular?

ALT4: ... that " [LINK: (Es hat sich halt eröffnet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Es_hat_sich_halt_er%C3%B6ffnet] ", a traditional 18th-century  [LINK: (Christmas carol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas_carol] , uses dialect words from  [LINK: (Tyrol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrol]  and  [LINK: (Swabia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swabia]  such as "purzigagalan" (somersaults)?

A full review is needed here for ALT1-4. ALT0 has been struck due to the aforementioned DYKFICTION concerns.  [LINK: (Narutolovehinata5) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Narutolovehinata5]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Narutolovehinata5]  ·  [LINK: (contributions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Contributions/Narutolovehinata5] )  [LINK: (03:20, 11 December 2025 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Narutolovehinata5-20251211032000-Gerda_Arendt-20251206215900] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]

ALT5a ... that " [LINK: (Es hat sich halt eröffnet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Es_hat_sich_halt_er%C3%B6ffnet] " is an 18th-century Christmas carol from both  [LINK: (Tyrol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrol] , Austria, and  [LINK: (Swabia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swabia] , Germany?

ALT5b ... that " [LINK: (Es hat sich halt eröffnet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Es_hat_sich_halt_er%C3%B6ffnet] " is an 18th-century Christmas carol that uses dialect words from  [LINK: (Tyrol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrol] , Austria, and  [LINK: (Swabia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swabia] , Germany?

@ [LINK: (Gerda Arendt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Gerda_Arendt] : The solution to that is ALT5c: ... that the 18th century Christmas carol " [LINK: (Es hat sich halt eröffnet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Es_hat_sich_halt_er%C3%B6ffnet] " is from the now-Austrian region Tyrol and the now-German region Swabia?. I see this is sourced to one offline source and two online sources, both of which imply Tyrol and Swabia are part of Austria and Germany but don't outright say it.-- [LINK: (Laun) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Launchballer]  [LINK: (chba) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Launchballer]  [LINK: (ller) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Contributions/Launchballer]   [LINK: (02:45, 23 December 2025 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Launchballer-20251223024500-Es_hat_sich_halt_eröffnet] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]

@ [LINK: (Launchballer) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Launchballer] : I've been thinking about this, and I wonder if ALT5a/ALT5b could be revisited. It seems like a good compromise between Gerda's desire to highlight the regions, and your desire to highlight the countries, while  avoiding the clunkiness of ALT5c. The issue is that "Tyrol" also includes a part in Italy, so saying "Tyrol, Austria" would be at best misleading (there is no such issue for Swabia, which as far as I can tell is entirely in present-day Germany). If the song originated from present-day Austria, then perhaps we can come up with a solution, but that is not clear in the sources. The safest option could be to just go with ALT5, if you prefer that angle, but it does mean it makes less obvious the "different countries" aspect you wanted to highlight.

How about using "Austrian Tyrol" and "German Swabia"?  [LINK: (Dahn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Dahn]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Dahn] )  [LINK: (17:50, 3 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Dahn-20260103175000-Launchballer-20251223024500] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]
I don't remember if the conversation had already that when the song was texted there was neither today's Germany nor today's Austria. Also: all the territory things say nothing about the topic. We sang it today with several people who didn't know it and we had a lot of fun that is missing in talking about locations only. -- [LINK: (Gerda Arendt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Gerda_Arendt]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Gerda_Arendt] )  [LINK: (23:11, 3 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Gerda_Arendt-20260103231100-Dahn-20260103175000] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]
Gerda, this is your comment from almost a month ago, on this very page: "and interesting that from two areas now in different countries, and even then disconnected".  [LINK: (Dahn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Dahn]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Dahn] )  [LINK: (07:40, 5 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Dahn-20260105074000-Gerda_Arendt-20260103231100] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]
At the time, I wanted Christmas Eve, and was willing to almost anything. We missed the chance. More interesting because more unique than any locations is the funny imagination (roll over backwards and forwards from heaven, offer cheese asking the baby). We seem to have guidelines that go for locations rather than fantasy, but is that a good thing? Can we have a bit of both, perhaps? Would we say about Beethoven's Fifth Symphony only where it was written? At this point, how about ALT3. -- [LINK: (Gerda Arendt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Gerda_Arendt]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Gerda_Arendt] )  [LINK: (10:16, 5 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Gerda_Arendt-20260105101600-Dahn-20260105074000] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]

