@ [LINK: (TonyFerro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:TonyFerro] : Article new enough (Dec 23), long enough (6100 B), no copyvio (Earwig says 5.7%). However, the sourcing has some issues, and I will be strict about this as the article deals with contentious topics. Please explain why the following sources are reliable: Georgios Koukakis, Greek Reporter, Eugene Kogan, MEPEI, Eurasia Review, Strategy International. Also,  [LINK: (there is a consensus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Reliable_sources/Noticeboard/Archive_347#Greek_City_Times]  that Greek City Times is unreliable, and the Agora Dialogue source is unusable ans it is merely a link to another source that's cited. In addition, it looks like there are a few sources that do not verify the stated information, such as Egypt Oil & Gas and Offshore Technology, which do not mention the alliance.There are some other, smaller issues (short sections, short lead, capitalized headings, duplicated references, typos, hyphen should be en dash), but these are simple stylistic issues that aren't required for the DYK criteria. As for the hook facts, ALT0 is interesting and verified in source. —  [LINK: (Vigilant Cosmic Penguin 🐧) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Vigilantcosmicpenguin]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Vigilantcosmicpenguin]  |  [LINK: (contribs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Contributions/Vigilantcosmicpenguin] )  [LINK: (23:37, 27 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Vigilantcosmicpenguin-20260127233700-TonyFerro-20251229141700] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]

