Free of  [LINK: (copyright violations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Copyright_violations] ,  [LINK: (plagiarism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Plagiarism] , and  [LINK: (close paraphrasing) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Close_paraphrasing] :

Hook eligibility:

[LINK: (Cited) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Did_you_know#Cited_hook] :  - There is a citation that supports the hook, but I'm not yet convinced it has enough weight to verify this kind of extraordinary claim. I can't find a lot about White Owl Books, seems like a somewhat niche publisher for what is appears to be a travel book. Similar to "first" claims, a healthy amount of caution around "only" claims is warranted, even if appears in a reliable source. I.e., how could/did Youngman verify the claim? Did she survey every distillery in Scotland before stating that Moffat was the only one?

Interesting:

Overall:  @ [LINK: (Grommmet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Grommmet] : Thanks for your work on the article! It is new enough (accepted via AFC on 6 Jan, 1 day before nom) and long enough (1700 B). EW 2.9% and no copyvio evident in online sources I spotchecked. I think the hook is interesting, but sourcing for the central claim (in hook and article) may need to be revisited. Otherwise, alternative hooks can be put forward for consideration. Best,  [LINK: (Zzz plant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Zzz_plant]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Zzz_plant] )  [LINK: (18:22, 10 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Zzz_plant-20260110182200-Moffat_distillery] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]

