Given the request for hook ideas, here are a few:

ALT1 ... that a lyric from the song " [LINK: (Nun ruhen alle Wälder) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun_ruhen_alle_W%C3%A4lder] " was described as ridiculous during the  [LINK: (Age of Enlightenment) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_of_Enlightenment] ?

ALT2 ... that  [LINK: (Frederick the Great) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_the_Great]  considered " [LINK: (Nun ruhen alle Wälder) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun_ruhen_alle_W%C3%A4lder] " a stupid song?

ALT2a ... that  [LINK: (Frederick the Great) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_the_Great]  cited " [LINK: (Nun ruhen alle Wälder) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun_ruhen_alle_W%C3%A4lder] " as an example of a stupid song?

Gerda, what you are trying to do with ALT1 is great, but I want to see it combined with ALT2.  Let me think about how to best do this.  [LINK: (Viriditas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Viriditas]  ( [LINK: (talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:Viriditas] )  [LINK: (22:07, 28 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-Viriditas-20260128220700-Nun_ruhen_alle_Wälder] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]

17th century: an evening song is written, from the perspective of a childlike person, mixing observations and reflection, successful in many hymnals.

18th century: the song gets ridiculed because the observations are read as if they were science, and the "foolish" song gets barely tolerated.

19th century: the song gets translated into English, matching romantic and spiritual interests.

20th century: the song gets interesting even for secular collections, as in the right mood for folk singing, making it popular.

21st century: the song finally enters a Catholic hymnal (which is an almost quirky final twist).

ALT4a ... that a  [LINK: (1647 evening song by Paul Gerhardt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun_ruhen_alle_W%C3%A4lder]  once mocked as foolish by  [LINK: (Frederick the Great) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_the_Great]  was added to a Catholic hymnal in 2013?

ALT5 ... that  [LINK: (Frederick the Great) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_the_Great]  once mocked the evening song " [LINK: (Now all the woods are sleeping) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun_ruhen_alle_W%C3%A4lder] " as foolish?

ALT4b ... that " [LINK: (Now all the woods are sleeping) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun_ruhen_alle_W%C3%A4lder] ", a 1647 evening song by  [LINK: (Paul Gerhardt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Gerhardt]  once mocked as foolish by Frederick the Great, was added to a Catholic hymnal in 2013?

@ [LINK: (Viriditas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Viriditas] : Throwing some more ideas here. For meeting in the middle, ALT4b is one option, since it mentioned the adding to the Catholic hymnal fact which she liked. On the other hand, we could also try something like:

ALT5 ... that  [LINK: (Catherine Winkworth) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_Winkworth]  translated into English a  [LINK: (1647 evening song by Paul Gerhardt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun_ruhen_alle_W%C3%A4lder]  once mocked as foolish by Frederick the Great?

ALT5a ... that " [LINK: (Nun ruhen alle Wälder) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun_ruhen_alle_W%C3%A4lder] ", a 1647 Paul Gerhardt song translated to English by  [LINK: (Catherine Winkworth) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_Winkworth] , was once mocked as foolish by Frederick the Great?

ALT5b ... that  [LINK: (Catherine Winkworth) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_Winkworth]  translated " [LINK: (Nun ruhen alle Wälder) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun_ruhen_alle_W%C3%A4lder] ", a 1647 Paul Gerhardt song once mocked as foolish by Frederick the Great, to English under the title "Now all the woods are sleeping"?

