Overall:  Where do I even start here. The hook idea here isn't cited or mentioned in the article whatsoever, which would make this hook misleading for guiding readers to a page that doesn't discuss this at all. As far as I am aware, this is an immediate failure. The article isn't neutrally worded at all; just in the lead alone, we have "It was amplified at the beginning of January 2026 when Grammy nominated Rapper, Fetty Wap’s publicist, Abesi PR posted “2016 is back.” Days later Fetty Wap re-emerged in New York after a three years hiatus. Tik Tok users instantly began posting 2026 is really 2016. TikTok did an instant reset to 2016. Almost overnight, TikTok began to sound and feel like a throwback 2016 reel". Confusing and unencyclopedic, especially that last sentence. The article cites  [LINK: (Hello! Magazine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hello!_Magazine] , which if my source reliability highlighter script is to be believed, is not a reliable source (though I can't find an entry for it on  [LINK: (WP:RSP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:RSP] ). The image in the article doesn't seem to be relevant to the text really at all (see  [LINK: (WP:IMAGEREL) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Wikipedia:IMAGEREL&action=edit&redlink=1] ). The phrase "described the year as "golden" in her posts" doesn't have a source at the end of it and is also grammatically incorrect considering that it refers to multiple people. I also don't think that the hook used here, even if it were cited in the article, is that interesting of a fact. This should not appear on the main page. λ  [LINK: (Negative) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:NegativeMP1]  [LINK: (MP1) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_talk:NegativeMP1]   [LINK: (00:28, 19 January 2026 (UTC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know#c-NegativeMP1-20260119002800-2026_is_the_new_2016] [ [LINK: (reply) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Did_you_know] ]

